\subsection{Adaptations of Existing Intermediate Results (Lemma \ref{lem4} and Lemma \ref{lem5})} \label{sec:adaptations}

In this section, we provide details on the modifications we make to the results in \cite{coja2020optimal, bay2022optimal} to obtain Lemmas \ref{lem4} and \ref{lem5}.  
Throughout the section, we will use symbols with a tilde (e.g., $\Tilde{n}$)  to denote quantities in a ``denser'' scaling regime, which will then be related to ``sparser'' regimes with the usual notation (e.g., $n$).  When the two settings both have the same value of a certain parameter, the tilde will be omitted (e.g., $k$).

\subsubsection{Establishing Lemma \ref{lem4}}

It is shown in \cite[Proof of Corollary 3.13]{coja2020optimal} that if $T \leq \big(\frac{1}{\log2}k\log_{2}k\big)(1-\epsilon)$, and if there are $k = \Tilde{n}^{\theta'}$ defective items in a population of size $\Tilde{n}$ for some $\theta'$ sufficiently close to one such that $2(1-\theta') < \xi <\epsilon\theta'$ for arbitrarily small $\xi > 0$, the number of masked defective items $\widetilde{M}_{1}$ is stochastically dominated by $\bin(\Tilde{n}^{1-\xi}, \frac{1}{3}\Tilde{n}^{\epsilon \theta' -1})$.  We then have
\begin{align}
    \label{eq:stoch_dom_1} \bP\Big(\widetilde{M}_{1} \leq \frac{1}{4}\tilde{n}^{\epsilon \theta' - \xi}\Big) \leq \bP\Big(\bin\Big(\tilde{n}^{1-\xi}, \frac{1}{3} \tilde{n}^{\epsilon \theta' -1}\Big) \leq \frac{1}{4}\tilde{n}^{\epsilon\theta' - \xi}\Big) = o(1)
\end{align}
by standard binomial concentration (Appendix \ref{app:bino}).  Since rearranging $k = \Tilde{n}^{\theta'}$ gives $\tilde{n} = k^{\frac{1}{\theta}}$, we have
\begin{equation}
    \label{eq:modified_stoch_dom} 
    \widetilde{M}_{1} \leq \frac{1}{4}\tilde{n}^{\epsilon \theta' - \xi} \iff \widetilde{M}_{1} \leq \frac{1}{4}k^{\epsilon - \frac{\xi}{\theta'}}.
    % \bP\left(\widetilde{M}_{1} \leq \frac{1}{4}k^{\epsilon - \frac{\xi}{\theta'}}\right) \leq \bP\left(\bin(k^{\frac{1}{\theta'}(1 - \xi)}, \frac{1}{3}k^{\epsilon - \frac{1}{\theta'}}) \leq \frac{1}{4}k^{\epsilon - \frac{\xi}{\theta'}}\right).
\end{equation}
Thus, with this parametrization, there are at least $\widetilde{M}_{1} \ge \frac{1}{4}k^{\epsilon - \frac{\xi}{\theta'}}$ masked defective items, with probability $1-o(1)$. 

For context, we now outline how converse results are translated from high $\theta'$ (near 1) to smaller $\theta$ in \cite{coja2020optimal}.  This is proved via the contrapositive statement that achievability for small $\theta$ implies the same for large $\theta'$ (with the same number of tests, say $T = c k \log k$ for some $c > 0$ when the two problems have the same $k$ but differing population sizes).  To show this, the denser problem with parameter $\theta'$ and $\tilde{n}$ items is solved by adding ``dummy non-defectives'' to increase the population size to $n \gg \tilde{n}$ (satisfying $n = \Theta(k^{\frac{1}{\theta}})$), without changing any of the test results, and the resultant sparser problem with parameter $\theta$ and $n$ items is then solved.  Note that the full argument makes use of Lemma \ref{lem3} and the set of satisfying sets $\cV$ therein; the optimal MAP decoder samples uniformly from $\cV$, and this set is unchanged by the addition of dummy non-defectives.


% \cite[Proposition 3.5]{coja2020optimal} states that for exact recovery, if a group testing algorithm succeeds with $T \leq \big(\frac{1}{\log 2}k\log_{2}k\big)(1-\epsilon)$ in finding $k$ defectives in a population of $n$ items, where $k = \Theta(n^{\theta})$ for some $\theta \in \big(\frac{\log 2}{1 + \log 2}, \theta')$, then there is a group testing algorithm that succeeds in finding $k$ defectives in a population of $\tilde{n} \coloneqq k^{\frac{1}{\theta'}}$ with the same number of tests. They prove this by using Lemma \ref{lem3} and showing that if for such a sparsity parameter $\theta$ there exists a test design $\sfX \in \{0,1\}^{T \times n}$ and decoding algorithm such that $\bP(\abs{\cV(\sfX, \bfY)} \leq t) \leq a$ (see Lemma \ref{lem3} for the definition of $\cV$) for some $t \geq 0$ and $a \in [0,1]$, then for the sparsity parameter $\theta'$ there exists a test design $\sfX' \in \{0,1\}^{T\times \tilde{n}}$ and decoding algorithm such that $\bP(\abs{\cV(\sfX', \bfY')} \leq t) \le a$, where $\bfY'$ is the set of test results induced by $\sfX'$. The intuition behind this is that we can translate an achievability result with $T$ tests from the high $\theta'$ regime to the low $\theta$ regime by adding in ``dummy non-defective" items. More specifically, non-defective items are added such that the population size increases from $\tilde{n} \coloneqq k^{\frac{1}{\theta'}}$ to $n$. Doing this does not affect any test outcomes, and \cite[Lemma 3.15]{coja2020optimal} shows that this can only increase the size of $\cV$ in the lower $\theta$ regime. Hence, achievability in this lower $\theta$ regime implies achievability in the higher $\theta'$ regime, since the optimal MAP estimator involves returning an item from $\cV$ chosen uniformly at random \cite[Fact 3.2]{coja2020optimal}. Taking the contrapositive, this means that if no group testing algorithm succeeds in the high $\theta'$ regime with $T = c k \log k$, then this implies the same for the lower $\theta$ re