\subsection{G2A Channel Model}



Firstly, the ground-to-air (G2A) channel model mainly depends on the BS's antenna gain, and the experienced path loss, and line-of-sight (LoS) probability \cite{3gpp777}. 



In cellular‐connected aerial systems, UAVs depend on the sidelobe emissions of BS antennas to establish links with terrestrial infrastructure. Consequently, an accurate representation of BS 3D radiation characteristics is essential for reliable UAV connectivity. 
We adopt the 3GPP antenna pattern specification \cite{3gpp777}, which closely approximates real‐world radiation behavior. Conforming to this model, each BS is partitioned into three sectors, each armed with cross‐polarized elements arranged in a uniform linear array (ULA).
Every antenna element delivers a peak gain of $B_{\max}=8$ dBi along its principal lobe \cite{3gpp777}, while sidelobe gains vary according to the UAV’s azimuth and elevation angles relative to the array \cite{cherif2022cellular}:
\begin{equation}
    B_{\mathrm{az}}(\phi^{ij}_t) =\min \left\{ 12 \left( {\phi^{ij}_t}/{\phi_\mathrm{3dB}} \right),\mathrm{B_m}  \right\},
\end{equation}
\begin{equation}
    B_{\mathrm{el}}(\zeta^{ij}_t) =\min \left\{ 12 \left( {\zeta^{ij}_t}/{\zeta_\mathrm{3dB}} \right), \mathrm{SLA}  \right\},
\end{equation}
where $\phi^{ij}_t$ and $\zeta^{ij}_t$ are the azimuth and elevation angles between BS $i$ and UAV $j$, and $\phi_\mathrm{3dB}=\zeta_\mathrm{3dB}=\frac{65\pi}{180}$ at 3dB bandwidths. $\mathrm{B_m} $ and $\mathrm{SLA}$ represent the antenna nulls thresholds, respectively. 
%
The, the gain of antenna element is
{\small
\begin{equation}
        B(\zeta^{ij}_t, \phi^{ij}_t) = B_{\mathrm{max}} 
    - \min \{- (B_{\mathrm{az}}(\phi^{ij}_t) + B_{\mathrm{el}}(\zeta^{ij}_t) ) , B_m \}.
\end{equation}}
Assuming that one BS equipped with $N$ antennas, and the array factor of the ULA of BS $i$ and UAV $j$ is
\begin{equation}
    \mathrm{F}(\zeta^{ij}_t) = \frac{\sin(\frac{N\pi}{2} (\sin \zeta^{ij}_t - \sin \zeta_i^d))}{\sqrt{N} \sin(\frac{\pi}{2} (\sin\zeta^{ij}_t - \sin\zeta_i^d))},
\end{equation}
where $\zeta_i^d$ is the down-tilt of BS $i$'s ULA. Finally, the array radiation pattern from BS $i$ to UAV $j$ is
\begin{equation}
B^{ij}_t = B(\zeta^{ij}_t,\phi^{ij}_t) + \mathrm{F}(\zeta^{ij}_t).
\end{equation}


Then, the probability of UAV $j$ has a LoS connection with BS $i$ depends on the altitude of the UAV and the surrounding environment, and the LoS probability is given by 
\begin{equation}
% \small
    P_{\mathrm{LoS}}(q^{ij}_t)= 
\begin{cases}
1,\quad &d^{ij}_t \leq d_1 \\
{d_1}/{d^{ij}_t} + e^{-\frac{d^{ij}_t}{p_1}}\left(1- {d_1}/{d^{ij}_t} \right),\quad &\text{otherwise,}
\end{cases} 
\end{equation}
where $d_1 = \max\{460\log_{10}(h_j)-700,18\}$ and $p_1 = 4300\log_{10}(h_j)-3800$. If $h_j \in [100,300]$ m, we assume the LoS probability is 1.
%
For the path loss between BS $i$ and UAV $j$, the probabilistic mean path loss value is
\begin{flalign}
    L^{ij}_t = L_i^{\mathrm{LoS}}P_{\mathrm{LoS}}(r^{ij}_t) +L_i^{\mathrm{NLoS}}P_{\mathrm{NLoS}}(r^{ij}_t),
\end{flalign}
where  $L_i^{\mathrm{LoS}}$ and $L_i^{\mathrm{NLoS}}$ indicate the LoS and NLoS path loss, respectively.
%
Finally, the average received power from BS $i$ is
\begin{equation}
    P^{ij}_t = P_T + B^{ij}_t - L^{ij}_t,
\end{equation}
where $P_T$ is BS transmit power. The BS-UAV link is determined by the strength of the received signal $P^{ij}_t$, and the related signal-to-interference-plus-noise-ratio (SINR) is
\begin{align}
            \text{SINR}^{ij}_t = \frac{P^{ij}_t}{\sum_{i'=1, i' \neq i}^{N_R}P^{i'j}_t + \sigma_0^2},
\end{align}
where $\sigma_0^2$ is the additive white Gaussian noise power.





\subsection{UAV-HAPS Channel Model}

We assume each UAV carries a single antenna~\cite{ren2023handoff}, whereas the HAPS platform employs an array of multiple antennas. 
Then the HAPS can generates a large number of spot‐beams.
%
We fix the carrier frequency at 2\,GHz for every UAV–HAPS connection and assign each link to an orthogonal channel. Given the total available bandwidth \(B_{\max}\), we seek an optimal partitioning among these channels: For UAV \(j\), the fraction of bandwidth allocated to its link with the HAPS is denoted \(b^{Hj}\), subject to $\sum_{j \in \mathcal{M}} b^{Hj} \le 1$.
%
Similarly, power distribution must respect the UAVs’ transmission constraints. Let \(P_{\max}\) be the maximum transmit power for any UAV; we denote the fraction of this power allocated to UAV \(j\)'s HAPS link by \(p^{Hj}\) and $0\le p^{Hj}\le1,\forall\,j \in \mathcal{M}$.




The UAV–HAPS links are modeled as LoS, and their large-scale attenuation follows the free-space path-loss model. Accordingly, the instantaneous channel gain can be written as \cite{alsharoa2020improvement}:
\begin{equation}\label{eq:channel_gain}
    B_{t}^{Hj}
    = B  \biggl(\frac{c}{4\pi\,d_{t}^{Hj}\,f_{c}}\biggr)^{2}
    \bigl|h^{Hj}\bigr|^{2},
\end{equation}
where \(c\) denotes the speed of light, \(d_{t}^{Hj}\) is the UAV-HASP distance, and \(f_{c}\) is the carrier frequency. \(B\) represents the directional antenna gain, and \(h^{Hj}\) is the small-scale Rician‐fading coefficient accounting for the LoS component.

Therefore, the resulting transmission rate over the UAV–HAPS link is 
\begin{equation}\label{eq:rate}
    R_{t}^{Hj}
    = b^{Hj}B_{\max}
    \log_{2}\!\biggl(
      1 \;+\;
      \frac{p^{Hj}P_{\max}\,B_{t}^{Hj}}
           {b^{Hj}B_{\max}\,N_{0}}
    \biggr).
\end{equation}





