\subsection{Weighted Data Rate with Handovers Constraints}

As UAVs navigate the aerial corridor, we distinguish two categories of handoffs: 1) Horizontal handoff: the UAV’s link is transferred between two terrestrial BSs; 2) Vertical handoff: the UAV’s connection shifts between terrestrial BSs and the HAPS.
%
Excessive handoff events can degrade the UAV’s achievable data rate, owing to the delays and potential failures associated with each transition. 
%
We assume each BS can access the possible UAV associations at each time instance, denoted by $n_i$. Then, based on the quota of each BS $i$, $Q_i \in [Q_R, Q_H]$, each UAV computes a \textit{weighted data rate metric} that encourages traffic load balancing and discourages unnecessary handoffs:
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:weight_data_rate}
    \text{WR}^{ij}_{t} = \frac{R^{ij}_{t}}{\min \left(Q_i, n_i \right)} (1 - \mu),
\end{equation}
where $\mu$ denotes the coefficient to discourage unnecessary handoffs. For instance, $\mu=0$ if keeping the previous BS, and $\mu=0.5$ if switching from terrestrial BS to HAPS and its traversal.






\section{Collaborative LLM Framework for Multi-UAV Control}
\label{sec:collab-llm}
% {In this section, we formulate the considered problem as a Multi objective Markov Decision Process (MOMDP) and discuss the corresponding design of state, action, and reward.
% }

% The state transitions and rewards are a function of the  UAV  environment and actions taken by the UAV.
As depicted in Fig.~\ref{fig:bdq_agent}, we employ a hierarchical two-level control architecture in which two collaborative LLM-based agents coordinate the UAV networks. The first LLM resides on the HAPS as a meta-controller overseeing global network operations. The second LLM is deployed at the network edge on each UAV, handling that individual UAV’s real-time motion decisions. The HAPS-level LLM continuously monitors the overall system state, including the total available HAPS bandwidth and each UAV’s instantaneous data rate, and issues high-level directives to the UAVs. In turn, the on-board UAV LLM controllers execute fine-grained flight control actions (e.g., speed and lane adjustments) in accordance with the meta-controller’s directives, while also responding to local environment conditions. This two-tier LLM framework enables cooperative decision-making across meta-control and edge-control levels, addressing both communication network performance and aerial traffic safety in a unified manner.

\subsection{HAPS Meta Decision Making Phase}

At the meta–control layer, a LLM deployed on the HAPS functions as a {global network orchestrator}.  Whenever the aggregate demand on the HAPS backhaul approaches its capacity, the LLM selects a subset of UAVs with the poorest HAPS link quality and instructs them to hand over to terrestrial BSs, thereby relieving congestion.  Once bandwidth becomes available, the meta‑controller authorizes the offloaded UAVs to re‑associate with the HAPS.  This policy maximizes the number of UAVs benefiting from the high‑throughput HAPS link while avoiding capacity violations and unnecessary handovers.

The decision process is modelled as an MDP 
$(\mathcal{S}_{\text{meta}},\mathcal{A}_{\text{meta}},\mathcal{P}_{\text{meta}},r_{\text{meta}})$.  
The state $\mathbf{s}_{\text{meta}}\in\mathcal{S}_{\text{meta}}$ captures the current HAPS load, per‑UAV data rates, and the availability of ground BS coverage.  
An action $\mathbf{a}_{\text{meta}}\in\mathcal{A}_{\text{meta}}$ specifies the set of UAVs to offload or recall.  
The transition kernel $\mathcal{P}_{\text{meta}}$ reflects the evolution of HAPS load and individual link qualities following these handovers.  
The reward of the HAPS is defined as
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:meta}
    r_{\text{meta}} = \underbrace{\eta_1\,\sum_{j \in \mathcal{M}}\text{WR}^{ij}_{t} - \eta_2\,\text{Sat}_{\text{HAPS}}}_{\text{throughput and saturation}} 
                 - \underbrace{\eta_3\,\mu}_{\text{handover penalty}},
\end{equation}
where the first term $\sum_{j \in \mathcal{M}}\text{WR}^{ij}_{t}$ weights the total throughput, the second term $\text{Sat}_{\text{HAPS}} \in \{0,1\}$ penalizes HAPS saturation events, and the third term $\eta_3\,\mu$ imposes a cost proportional to the number of enforced handovers. The LLM therefore learns a policy that maintains load balance, sustains high throughput, and minimizes disruptive switching.  
The overall procedure is summarized in Algorithm~\ref{alg:meta_controller}.

% At the meta-control level, the HAPS-based LLM acts as a central network meta-controller. When the aggregate demand on the HAPS link approaches or exceeds its capacity, the meta-controller’s policy determines a subset of UAVs that must be offloaded from the HAPS to prevent overload. In particular, the meta-controller identifies UAVs with the lowest current data rates or less critical link requirements and instructs them to descend to lower altitudes and hand over their connection to terrestrial BSs. By offloading these lower-performing UAVs, the HAPS frees up bandwidth for the remaining high-performing UAVs, thereby avoiding capacity saturation. Conversely, once the HAPS has sufficient available bandwidth, the meta-controller signals the previously offloaded UAVs to reascend and reconnect to the HAPS link. This dynamic association strategy ensures that as many UAVs as possible benefit from the HAPS’s high-throughput link when capacity permits, while gracefully degrading to local BS connectivity when the HAPS is at its limit. Notably, the meta-LLM’s decisions also consider handover costs and energy efficiency—excessive or unnecessary descents are discouraged since any UAV that drops to a BS must later climb back to rejoin the HAPS when capacity allows. Thus, UAVs are only sent to the ground network when absolutely necessary, and they remain hovering at altitude (connected to HAPS) whenever possible to avoid needless handovers.

% We model the HAPS meta-controller’s decision-making as a Markov Decision Process (MDP). The meta-level state $\mathbf{s}{\text{meta}}$ includes global network information such as the current number of UAVs served by the HAPS, the fraction of HAPS bandwidth in use, each UAV’s recent data rate or link quality to the HAPS, and the availability of terrestrial BS connectivity for each UAV (e.g., which UAVs are in range of which ground BSs).
% %
%  Based on this state, the meta-controller LLM chooses an action $\mathbf{a}{\text{meta}}$ specifying a network reconfiguration—namely, which UAVs (if any) should be switched from the HAPS link to a terrestrial BS (or permitted to switch back to HAPS). For example, an action might be $\mathbf{a}_{\text{meta}} = {\text{offload }u_i, u_j;~ \text{keep others}}$, 
%  indicating that UAVs $u_i$ and $u_j$ are instructed to descend and associate with ground BSs, while all other UAVs remain connected to HAPS. The state transition $\mathcal{P}_{\text{meta}}(\mathbf{s}_{t+1}|\mathbf{s}_t,\mathbf{a}_{t})$ captures how the network evolves after these commands: when certain UAVs hand over to BSs, the HAPS load in the next time slot drops (releasing bandwidth) and those UAVs’ link qualities change according to the terrestrial channel; if later the meta-controller calls them back, the opposite transition occurs. The meta-controller’s reward $r_{\text{meta}}$ is designed to reflect the global communication performance. A positive reward is given for states with high total network throughput and balanced load (no single link overloaded), while penalties are assigned for HAPS saturation events (demand exceeding capacity) or poor link quality for any UAV. The reward also incorporates a penalty term for each handover action (forcing a UAV to switch networks), to minimize excessive switching and ensure stability. Through this reward structure, the meta-level LLM learns a policy that maximizes overall data delivery while minimizing disruptive handovers and inefficient use of resources.
%  The HAPS LLM meta controller is summarized in \textbf{Algorithm~1}

\begin{algorithm}[ht]
\caption{Meta-controller LLM for UAV Network Reconfiguration}
\label{alg:meta_controller}
\SetKwInOut{Input}{Input}
\SetKwInOut{Output}{Output}

\Input{Initial meta-state $\mathbf{s}_\text{meta}$, UAV set $\mathcal{U}$, HAPS bandwidth limit $C$, ground BS availability, threshold parameters}
\Output{Optimal UAV association actions $\mathbf{a}_\text{meta}$}
Initialize LLM parameters and policy network $\pi_\text{meta}$\;
\For{each timestep $t$}{
    Observe current meta-state $\mathbf{s}_t$ (UAV data rates, HAPS load, BS availability)\;\\
    Compute bandwidth usage: \\ $B_t \leftarrow \sum_{j \in \mathcal{J}} \text{WR}^{ij}_{t}$\;\\
    \eIf{$B_t > C$}{
        Identify UAV $j$ with lowest data rates or least critical tasks\;\\
        Action $\mathbf{a}_t \leftarrow$ Offload UAV $j$ to terrestrial BSs\;\\
    }{
        Identify offloaded UAV eligible for reattachment to HAPS\;\\
        Action $\mathbf{a}_t \leftarrow$ Reattach UAV $j$ to HAPS\;\\
    }
    Execute action $\mathbf{a}_t$\;\\
    UAVs update associations accordingly\;\\
    Observe next meta-state $\mathbf{s}_{t+1}$ and compute reward $r_{t}$ by Equation (\ref{eq:meta})
% $r_t \leftarrow \alpha \cdot (\text{Throughput}) - \beta \cdot (\text{HAPS overload}) - \Delta \cdot (\text{Poor link quality}) - \delta \cdot (\text{Number of handovers})$\;
\\
    Update policy $\pi_\text{meta}$ using $(\mathbf{s}_t, \mathbf{a}_t, r_t, \mathbf{s}_{t+1})$\;\\
}
\Return learned optimal association policy $\pi^*_\text{meta}$\;
\end{algorithm}




\subsection{UAV Motion and Telecommunication Action Phase}

At the \textbf{edge-control} layer, each UAV embeds an onboard LLM responsible for real-time 3D trajectory and radio access decisions. In addition to longitudinal speed adjustments and lateral lane shifts, the UAV may alter its altitude whenever authorized by the HAPS meta-controller, yielding a unified horizontal–vertical maneuver set. Consequently, each UAV addresses its own Markov decision process (MDP), whose action space is influenced by the HAPS controller, while state transitions and rewards are observed locally.

\subsubsection{State Space}
Let $M_1$ denote the number of target UAVs within the decision horizon. Each UAV $j$ at time $t$ is characterized by
\[
\bigl\{x^j_t,\,y^j_t,\,z^j_t,\,v^j_t,\,\psi^j_t,\,n^{R,j}_t,\,n^{H,j}_t\bigr\},
\]
where $(x^j_t,y^j_t,z^j_t)$ is the 3D position, $v^j_t$ the forward velocity, $\psi^j_t$ the heading angle, and $n^{R,j}_t$ ($n^{H,j}_t$) the count of terrestrial BSs (HAPS channels) within service range that meet the target rate. Stacking these row vectors for all $j=1,\dots,M_1$ forms the global state
\[
\mathcal{S}_t =
\begin{bmatrix}
x^1_t & y^1_t & z^1_t & v^1_t & \psi^1_t & n^{R,1}_t & n^{H,1}_t \\[-1pt]
\vdots & \vdots & \vdots & \vdots & \vdots & \vdots & \vdots \\[-1pt]
x^{M_1}_t & y^{M_1}_t & z^{M_1}_t & v^{M_1}_t & \psi^{M_1}_t & n^{R,M_1}_t & n^{H,M_1}_t
\end{bmatrix}\!.
\]

\subsubsection{Two-Dimensional Action Space}\label{subsection:2daction}
At each decision instant, a \textbf{joint} action $(a_{\rm tele},a_{\rm tran})\in\mathcal{A}_{\rm tele}\times\mathcal{A}_{\rm tran}$ is selected. The set
\[
\mathcal{A}_{\rm tran}=\{a_{\rm tran}^1,\dots,a_{\rm tran}^5\}
\]
corresponds to \textbf{left-lane change}, \textbf{keep lane}, \textbf{right-lane change}, \textbf{accelerate}, and \textbf{decelerate}. Acceleration values follow the kinematic model in~\cite{yan2023multi}, so identical symbolic actions can produce different numeric rates.

The telecommunication choices
\[
\mathcal{A}_{\rm tele}=\{a_{\rm tele}^1,a_{\rm tele}^2,a_{\rm tele}^3\}
\]
denote BS selection strategies: $a_{\rm tele}^1$ selects the BS maximizing $\text{WR}_{ij}$ in~\eqref{eq:weight_data_rate}; $a_{\rm tele}^2$ uses the same metric with load threshold $\mu=0$, iteratively moving to the next candidate if saturated; and $a_{\rm tele}^3$ opts for the BS offering the highest instantaneous rate $R_{ij,t}$.

\subsubsection{Reward Design}
The transportation reward for UAV $j$ is
\begin{equation}
r^{\mathrm{j,tran}}_{t}=
w_1\!\Bigl(\frac{v^j_t-v_{\min}}{v_{\max}-v_{\min}}\Bigr)
- w_2\,\delta_c
- w_3\,\chi^j_t,
\end{equation}
where $\delta_c\in\{0,1\}$ indicates a collision and $\chi^j_t$ is the ratio of total lane changes to elapsed time. Negative returns are avoided to discourage premature termination.

For communications, the reward is
\begin{equation}
r^{\mathrm{j,tele}}_{t}=
w_4\,\text{WR}_{i^*,j,t}\bigl(1-\min(1,\xi^j_t)\bigr),
\end{equation}
where $\xi^j_t$ is the empirical handover probability up to time $t$. The weights $w_1,\dots,w_4$ prioritize safety ($w_2$) and connectivity ($w_4$) above secondary objectives.

Although the HAPS meta-controller operates on a slower timescale, its altitude offloading commands restrict each UAV’s vertical choices and, via $n^{H,j}_t$, influence the local state and rewards. This hierarchical LLM framework jointly optimizes network utilization and collision-free traffic flow, yielding a Pareto-efficient trade-off among throughput, handover overhead, and flight safety. 



