The prompt is organised into six intuitive blocks from the viewpoint of a \textbf{UAV decision agent}:

\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Task Description} declares the overarching mission: maintain high–speed, collision‑free flight along the aerial highway.
    \item \textbf{Task Goal} – details the three optimisation targets for each UAV: (i) maximise forward velocity, (ii) avoid collisions, and (iii) minimise unnecessary lateral manoeuvres.
    \item \textbf{State Definition} – enumerates all environmental features inspected by the agent, namely the 3‑D position, velocity, heading, and the counts of viable \textbf{BS}/\textbf{HAPS} links.
    \item \textbf{Observations} – present a discretised post‑processing matrix of those features for the ego UAV and surrounding aircraft.
    \item \textbf{Experience Memory} – supplies two reference tables (``good’’ vs.\ ``bad’’ cases) that offer precedents to guide the LLM when it encounters unseen states.
    \item \textbf{Reply Rules} – constrain the model’s output to exactly one transport action and one telecom policy selected from the predefined action set, ensuring decisions remain focused and comparable across time‑steps.
\end{enumerate}

This structured natural‑language template encapsulates the entire optimisation problem while remaining fully compatible with the meta‑controller logic in Algorithm~1.

LLM agent respond is
% ---------- Sample LLM Reply ----------
\begin{mdframed}
The ego UAV is already centred in its lane and moving at a moderate speed (\texttt{vx}=3).  
Vehicle 2 ahead is slower, but there is ample gap on its left; a controlled acceleration keeps throughput high without risking collision.

For telecom, the load is light (\texttt{gbs\_cnt}=2, \texttt{haps\_cnt}=1) and recent good experiences indicate \texttt{t1} yields higher long‑term reward when HO penalties matter.

\verb|<tran_action>FASTER</tran_action>|

\verb|<tele_action>t1</tele_action>|
\end{mdframed}
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Edge deployment via \textsc{Ollama}.}  
    The LLM is hosted on nearby edge servers using the \textsc{Ollama} framework.  
    Locating inference close to the autonomous vehicles eliminates wide‑area round‑trip delays, keeping response times within the strict real‑time bounds required for autonomous driving.

    \item \textbf{Targeted experience prompts.}  
    To curb computational load, the agent supplies the LLM with only the five \textbf{most similar} \textbf{good} and \textbf{bad} experiences, determined by Euclidean distance in state space, rather than the entire replay buffer.  
    This focused context preserves decision quality while markedly accelerating inference.
\end{itemize}
