Given a graph $G$, a positive integer $\tau$ and a function $\lambda: E(G) \rightarrow \mathcal{P} ([\tau])$, with $\mathcal{P}([\tau])$ being the power set of $\{1,\dots,\tau\}$, such that each edge is assigned a finite non-empty subset of $[\tau]$. 
Then $\mathcal{G} = (G,\lambda)$ is a \emph{temporal graph} with \emph{lifetime} $\tau$.
%\end{definition}
%
We can see the vertices and edges of $\mathcal{G}$ in two ways. One is to see them as just the vertices and edges of $G$. The other is to add a temporal component to them. In this way, we have \emph{temporal vertices} in the form $(v,i)\in V(G)\times [\tau]$, and \emph{temporal edges} in the form $(e,j)$ with $e\in E(G)$ and $j\in\lambda(e)$.
