\begin{abstract}
Ensuring safety of large language models (LLMs) is important. Red teaming—a systematic approach to identifying adversarial prompts that elicit harmful responses from target LLMs—has emerged as a crucial safety evaluation method. Within this framework, the diversity of adversarial prompts is essential for comprehensive safety assessments. We find that previous approaches to red-teaming may suffer from two key limitations. First, they often pursue diversity through simplistic metrics like word frequency or sentence embedding similarity, which may not capture meaningful variation in attack strategies. Second, the common practice of training a single attacker model restricts coverage across potential attack styles and risk categories. This paper introduces Quality-Diversity Red-Teaming (QDRT), a new framework designed to address these limitations. QDRT achieves goal-driven diversity through behavior-conditioned training and implements a behavioral replay buffer in an open-ended manner. Additionally, it trains multiple specialized attackers capable of generating high-quality attacks across diverse styles and risk categories. Our empirical evaluation demonstrates that QDRT generates attacks that are both more diverse and more effective against a wide range of target LLMs, including \texttt{GPT-2}, \texttt{Llama-3}, \texttt{Gemma-2}, and \texttt{Qwen2.5}. This work advances the field of LLM safety by providing a systematic and effective approach to automated red-teaming, ultimately supporting the responsible deployment of LLMs. 
\begin{center}
\color{red}{\textbf{Warning: This paper contains offensive language model outputs.}}    
\end{center}
\end{abstract}



\vspace{-0.4cm}
\section{Introduction}
\label{sec:introduction}

Recent advances in large language models (LLMs) have enabled their successful application across a wide range of scenarios \citep{long2022training, openai2023gpt4, dubey2024the}. As LLMs become increasingly integrated into real-world applications, ensuring their safety and responsible use has become a critical challenge~\cite{wang2025a}. One effective approach to addressing this issue is red-teaming~\cite{ganguli2022red,lin2025against,ethan2022red,zhouxing2024red,hong2024curiositydriven,lee2025learning,beutel2024diverse,mikayel2024rainbow}, a method designed to expose potential safety risks by identifying adversarial prompts that elicit harmful or unwanted outputs from LLMs~\cite{bai2022constitutional,zou2023universal}. Identifying diverse prompts presents both scientific intrigue and practical significance,  which this paper explores. 

Human-driven red-teaming has proven effective in identifying vulnerabilities~\cite{dinan2019build,alexander2023jailbroken, shen2024}. However, it is inherently constrained by the limits of human creativity and the time-intensive nature of manual prompt engineering~\cite{ethan2022red,hong2024curiositydriven}.
In contrast, automated red-teaming~\cite{ganguli2022red,ethan2022red}, which involves training an LLM to serve as a red-teamer, offers a promising direction to address these limitations by enabling more efficient discovery of potential adversarial prompts. Nevertheless, prior work about red-teaming often faces a trade-off between attack success rate and attack diversity~\cite{ethan2022red,hong2024curiositydriven,lee2025learning}. For one thing, zero-shot (or few-shot) prompting approaches (see e.g., ~\cite{ethan2022red}) allow for more diverse outputs but results in a lower attack success rate. While reinforcement learning (RL) training significantly improves attack success rates, it also leads to highly homogeneous prompts~\cite{beutel2024diverse}. For another thing, recent efforts~\cite{hong2024curiositydriven,lee2025learning} have attempted to address this issue, but at the cost of reduced attack success rates. Therefore, in this work, we aim to answer the following question:
\begin{center}
    \textit{How can we train a red-teaming LLM to generate attacks that are both diverse and effective?}
\end{center}




To achieve this goal, we first revisit the definition of diversity in red-teaming. Diversity is typically defined by simplistic metrics like word frequency or sentence embedding similarity in the mentioned work. Unfortunately, such a defined notion does not capture meaningful variation in attack strategies and risk categories effectively. Secondly, existing automated red-teaming methods typically rely on training a single attacker. This common practice restricts coverage across potential attack styles and risk categories, leading to inadequate safety evaluations. Recent works show that maintaining a set of policies could help improve the diversity, such as quality-diversity~\cite{antoine2015robots,cully2018quality,chatzilygeroudis2021qualitydiversity} and population-based training~\cite{thomas2023evolving}. However, how to obtain a set of diverse attackers is still an open problem.

