To address these issues, we first propose pursuing goal-driven diversity based on the attack’s actual behavior, namely risk category and attack style. Our objective is to train a set of effective attackers to generate attacks exhibiting diverse behaviors. We formulate this as a quality-diversity optimization problem~\cite{cully2018quality,chatzilygeroudis2021qualitydiversity} and propose the Quality-Diversity Red-Teaming (QDRT) framework. The QDRT framework comprises three main components: (1) an open-ended training process involving iterative parent selection, variation, and survival selection; (2) behavior-conditioned training to explicitly encourage specific behaviors, using this as a reward signal in the RL training process; and (3) a deep MAP-Elites (ME) grid buffer~\cite{mouret2015illuminating} to collect data during training, which achieves a better balance between data quality and diversity compared to a vanilla buffer.

To evaluate the performance of our proposed QDRT, we conduct experiments on a variety of LLMs, including \texttt{GPT-2}~\cite{long2022training}, \texttt{Llama-3.2-3B-Instruct}~\cite{dubey2024the}, \texttt{Gemma-2-2B-it}~\cite{team2024gemma}, \texttt{Gemma-2-9B-it}~\cite{team2024gemma}, \texttt{Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct}~\cite{dubey2024the}, and \texttt{Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct}~\cite{yang2024qwen2}. As shown in Figure~\ref{fig:fig1}, although starting from a low-quality set of attack examples, QDRT is able to generate a diverse set of attacks while maintaining a high toxicity rate.

Our contribution are three-folds:
\begin{itemize}
    \item Goal-driven diversity optimization. We introduce a new definition of diversity, which is based on structured behavior space of attack styles and risk categories, replacing simplistic metrics to align with real-world security threats.
    \item Effective optimization framework. We develop the QDRT framework, including behavior-conditioned training, behavior assignment strategy, and a MAP-Elites buffer to balance attack quality and diversity.
    \item Comprehensive validations. QDRT achieves 22.13\% QD-Score and 19.33\% behavior coverage improvement over state-of-the-art methods on various LLMs.
\end{itemize}


\section{Related Works}
\label{sec:relatedworks}

\paragraph{Red-teaming.} Consider a target LLM that generates a text response $y$ from a text prompt $x$ with probability $p(y \mid x)$. Red teaming~\cite{ethan2022red,zhouxing2024red,ganguli2022red,lin2025against} seeks to discover a diverse set of adversarial prompts $x$ that induce the target model $p$ to generate harmful outputs $y$. For toxicity evaluation, a judge model $r$ (e.g., \texttt{Llama-Guard-3}\footnote{\url{https://huggingface.co/meta-llama/Llama-Guard-3-8B}}) assigns a toxicity score $r(x, y)$, where higher values indicate greater severity of safety violations in response $y$. The goal of red-teaming algorithms is to find a diverse set of adversarial prompts $x$ that cause the target LLM to generate responses $y$ with high $r(x, y)$. \textbf{Jailbreaking}~\cite{alexander2023jailbroken,yi2024jailbreak,xu2024a} is an area closely associated with red-teaming, which shares substantial technical overlap in identifying LLM models' weaknesses. For clarity of exposition in subsequent discussions, we will treat these interrelated concepts as common technologies rather than enforcing rigid categorical distinctions.

\paragraph{Prompts-level red-teaming.}
Prompt-level attacks circumvent LLM defenses against generating undesirable responses by directly designing adversarial prompts. Early work relied on manual design~\cite{dinan2019build}, while recent approaches use black-box optimization algorithms for iterative optimization. For example, Lee et al.~\cite{lee2023queryefficient} employed Bayesian optimization to efficiently attack language models. Rainbow teaming~\cite{mikayel2024rainbow} uses an iterative evolutionary process to automate the discovery of a wide range of adversarial prompts. Ruby teaming~\cite{han2024ruby} improves Rainbow teaming by incorporating a memory cache as its third behavior feature. However, these approaches can suffer from mode collapse and poor transferability, often encountering limitations in scalability and adaptability, which necessitate adopting more sophisticated techniques like RL-based generators.

\paragraph{Generators-level red-teaming.}
As the space of adversarial prompts $x$ is too large, it is hard to directly search in it. Recent generators-level red-teaming algorithms train an adversarial prompt generator $\pi_\theta$ parameterized by $\theta$ to generate adversarial prompts $x$ with probability $\pi_\theta(x)$ automatically, allowing to generate novel prompts efficiently at test time and providing more flexibility and transferability. 
Then, the red-teaming problem can be formulated as an reinforcement learning problem \cite{ethan2022red,zhouxing2024red}, which typically uses the toxicity score $r(x, y)$ as the reward and trains the adversarial prompt generator to maximize the toxicity while minimizing the distance between the generator and a reference model $\pi_\text{ref}$ by reinforcement learning algorithms (e.g., \citep{schulman2017proximal,li2024remax,shao2024deepseekmath}):
\begin{equation}
    \label{eq:reinforce-reward}
    \max_\theta \mathbb E_{x \sim \pi_\theta(\cdot), y \sim p(\cdot \mid x)} [r(x, y) - \lambda \cdot D_\text{KL}(\pi_\theta(x) \| \pi_\text{ref}(x))],
\end{equation}
where $D_\text{KL}$ is the KL-divergence and $\lambda > 0$ is a hyper-parameter. Recently, Hong et al.~\cite{hong2024curiositydriven} used a novelty-based reward~\cite{lehman2011evolving} to encourage a policy to explore diverse samples during RL training; Lee et al.~\cite{lee2025learning} proposed to use GFlowNets~\cite{bengio2021flow} to improve quality and diversity of generated prompts, achieving the state-of-the-art results. However, these methods do not consider the diversity goal of the generated prompts, resulting in insufficient diversity in the attack styles and the risk categories of the generated prompts. 


\paragraph{Quality-diversity.}
Quality-Diversity (QD) algorithms~\citep{antoine2015robots,mouret2015illuminating,cully2018quality,chatzilygeroudis2021qualitydiversity}, which are a subset of evolutionary algorithms~\citep{back1996evolutionary,zhou2019evolutionary}, have emerged as a potent optimization paradigm for diversity optimization tasks. Specifically, a QD algorithm maintains a solution set (i.e., archive), and iteratively performs the following procedure: selecting a subset of parent solutions from the archive, applying variation operators (e.g., crossover and mutation) to produce offspring solutions, and finally using these offspring solutions to update the archive. The impressive performance of QD algorithms has been showcased in various RL tasks, such as exploration~\citep{ecoffet2021first,miao2022promoting}, robust training~\citep{saurabh2020one,paul2021learning,mikayel2024rainbow}, and environment generation~\citep{fontaine2021illuminating,varun2022deep,zhang2023multirobot}. Recent efforts have introduced QD into red-teaming, such as Rainbow Teaming~\cite{mikayel2024rainbow} and Ruby Teaming~\cite{han2024ruby}; however, they focus on the prompt level and do not consider how to train a effective generator.

\section{Method}

In this section, we introduce our proposed Quality-Diversity Red-Teaming (QDRT) framework. We first define the goal-driven diversity in Section~\ref{sec:3-1}, and then introduce the behavior-conditioned training and overall QDRT framework in Sections~\ref{sec:3-2} and \ref{sec:3-3}, respectively. 


\subsection{Goal-Driven Diversity}\label{sec:3-1}



\paragraph{How does existing methods define the diversity of attackers?} 
Popular diversity definition of generators-based red-teaming can be categorized as follows: \textbf{1) $n$-grams modeling}: BLEU score~\cite{papineni2002bleu} measures the $n$-gram overlaps between sentence $x$ and reference sentence set $\mathcal X$, and $\text{SelfBLEU}_\mathcal X(x, n)$~\cite{zhu2018texygen} uses all the $n$ prior sentences as the reference sentence set of BLEU score. Lower SelfBLEU scores indicate less overlaps and more diversity. Thus, negative averaged SelfBLEU score~\cite{hong2024curiositydriven} can be naturally used as the diversity measure.
\textbf{2) Sentence embedding}: To measure the embedding-level diversity, some prior works~\cite{hong2024curiositydriven,lee2025learning} use sentence embedding models to obtain low-dimensional embeddings of the sentences, and use the cosine distance between the embeddings as the diversity measure.

However, pursuing diversity based on these latent embedding-based methods does not necessarily lead to diversity in the final attack style and risk category.
Additionally, due to the lack of specific feedback information as a reward and as a replay data filter, the generator often struggles to cover the entire space. To the best of our knowledge, we are the first to use goal-driven diversity as the reward signal into the RL training process of generator-level red-teaming, which aligns well with the ultimate goals of red-teaming.

\paragraph{Goal-driven behavior.}
Diversity can be typically defined on a structured and semantically meaningful behavior space~\cite{cully2018quality,chatzilygeroudis2021qualitydiversity}. Then, the optimization objective is to find a set of high-quality solutions (i.e., attacks in red-teaming) with diverse behaviors.
The ultimate goal of red-teaming is to generate a series of attacks with actual semantic meaning, i.e., a certain harmful category with a specific attack style.
To achieve this goal and enable controllable attack generation~\cite{sumanth2020plug}, we define the goal-driven behavior based on attack's actual \textbf{risk category} and \textbf{attack style}, where the risk category is the category of the hazard that may be involved in LLM response, e.g., violent crimes, and the attack style is the jailbreaking strategy used by the attacker, e.g, role play.
Specifically, we use the same set of risk categories $\mathcal S$ as the \texttt{Llama-Guard-3-8B} model~\cite{inan2023llama,dubey2024the}, i.e., $\mathcal S = \{\text{``violent crimes''}, \text{``sex-related crimes''}, \dots\}$, and use an augmented set of attack styles $\mathcal C$ from Rainbow Teaming~\cite{mikayel2024rainbow}, i.e., $\mathcal C = \{\text{``role play''}, \text{``slang''}, \dots\}$. We use $\mathcal B$ to notate the whole behavior space, i.e., $\mathcal B = \mathcal S \times \mathcal C = \{(\text{``violent crimes''}, \text{``role play''}),(\text{``violent crimes''}, \text{``slang''}), \dots\}$. The details of goal-driven behavior definition are shown in Appendix A.1 and A.2.


\paragraph{Evaluation of goal-driven behavior.}
Given the definition of the goal-driven behaviors, we need to evaluate the behaviors of the generated adversarial prompts to enhance the behavioral diversity.
Recently, some pretrained judge models have been proposed to evaluate the risk categories of the adversarial prompts and their responses, such as Llama-Guard series~\cite{inan2023llama}.
However, there are no models for evaluating the attack styles yet.
In this work, we instruct another general purpose LLM to evaluate the attack style of the adversarial prompts.
Specifically, we use \texttt{Llama-Guard-3-8B}~\cite{inan2023llama,dubey2024the} to evaluate \textbf{risk category} and \textbf{toxicity score} of the adversarial prompts and their responses. Given an adversarial prompt $x$ and its response $y \sim p(\cdot \mid x)$ sampled from the target model, \texttt{Llama-Guard-3} returns one token of ``safe'' or ``unsafe'' and the ID of the risk category that the response potential violates. We use the probability of the ``unsafe'' token as the reward $r(x, y)$ and use the probability of token of each risk category $c$ as $p_\phi(c \mid x, y)$. Compared to risk category, \textbf{attack style} is more difficult to evaluate because there is currently no specialized model for identifying it. We first use a small judge model to evaluate the attack style, i.e., instructing a \texttt{Llama-3.2-3B-Instruct} model to act as the judge model for attack style. Given the adversarial prompt and a list of attack styles, the model is required to output the attack style of the prompt. The probability $p_\psi(s \mid x)$ is defined as the probability of the corresponding token of the attack style ID. The prompt we used can be found in Appendix~C.2. For better evaluation, we also use OpenAI \texttt{GPT-4.1} to act as the judge model for attack style. Given an adversarial prompt $x$, we prompt the judge model to output the attack style and use the probability of token of each attack style $s$ as $p_\psi(s \mid x)$. The prompt we used can be found in Appendix~C.3.


\subsection{Behavior-Conditioned Training}\label{sec:3-2}



Given the definition of the behavior space, the adversarial prompt generators are expected to generate diverse adversarial prompts that can cover the entire behavior space. To archive this,
the QDRT framework takes two steps: First, to enable the attacker model to follow the attack instructions and utilize the initial dataset, we performs a supervised fine-tuning (SFT) to the attacker model. Then, we train
the attacker models through behavior-conditioned reinforcement learning to improve the quality and generalization ability, and enable the attacker model to generate adversarial prompts with diverse behaviors that cover the entire behavior space. Note that QDRT is a general framework for red-teaming and can be implemented with arbitrary SFT and RL algorithms.

\paragraph{SFT.}
For each adversarial prompt $x$ in the initial dataset, QDRT obtains the target model's response $y \sim p(\cdot \mid x)$ and evaluates the corresponding behavior $(c, s)$. Subsequently, QDRT instructs the attacker model to generate adversarial prompts with behavior $(c, s)$, and fine-tunes it to maximize the probability of generating the adversarial prompt $x$. The instruction can be found in Appendix~C.1. We use GEM~\citep{li2025preserving} to fine-tune the attacker model instead of standard SFT because it is better at preserving the output diversity (Detailed results are shown in Table~\ref{tab:qd-score}).

\paragraph{RL training.}
In the RL training stage, QDRT samples a behavior tuple $(c, s)$ (i.e., a combination of a risk category and an attack style) at a time from the behavior space. Then, it instructs the generator to generate adversarial attacks $x \sim \pi_\theta(\cdot \mid c, s)$ with the given behavior. The generated attacks are then used to attack the victim model, resulting in responses $y \sim p(\cdot \mid x)$. The toxicity scores $r(x, y)$ and the probabilities $(p_\phi(c \mid x, y), p_\psi(s \mid x))$ of the behaviors of the attacks and responses are evaluated by the judge models. Subsequently, we define the behavior-conditioned score $r(c,s,x)$ as the probability that the attack $x$ is successful with the given goal-driven behavior $(c, s)$:
\begin{equation}
    \label{eq:bc-loss}
    r(c,s,x) = \mathbb E_{y \sim p(\cdot \mid x)}[p_\phi(c \mid x, y)  p_\psi(s \mid x) r(x, y)].
\end{equation}

The behavior-conditioned score can be estimated by sampling some responses from the victim model. It can then be used as the reward of the generator, which can be trained through various reinforcement learning algorithms. The behavior-conditioned training ensures that the attacker models are able to attack with diverse behaviors and cover the entire behavior space with high quality adversarial prompts.





\subsection{QDRT Framework}\label{sec:3-3}

To enhance the diversity of the generated adversarial attacks, QDRT trains multiple diverse attacker models that focus on different subspaces of the behavior space. At the beginning of the RL stage, the behavior space is divided by attack styles randomly and assigned to each attacker model. At each training step, each attacker model samples a behavior from the behavior subspace assigned to it
and conduct a behavior-conditioned variation on this behavior.
After a number of training steps, each attacker model is evaluated, and the behavior subspaces of the attacker models are reassigned accordingly.
Specifically, to improve the performance and the coverage of the behavior space, QDRT iteratively assigns each attacker model the unassigned attack style that it generated the most in the evaluation process, until all attack styles are assigned.

\paragraph{Overall framework.} The pseudo-code of QDRT is shown in Algorithm~\ref{alg:qdrt}. The attacker models are initialized in line~1 and the behavior space is randomly divided and assigned to the attacker models based on attack styles in line~2. At each training step, each attacker model samples a behavior $(c, s)$ from the assigned behavior subspace $\mathcal A_i$ in line~6. Then, QDRT performs on-policy or off-policy sampling, depending on the underlying reinforcement learning algorithm. In this work, we use GFlowNets as the underlying reinforcement learning algorithm~\cite{lee2025learning}. It randomly chooses between them with a probability of 0.5. For on-policy sampling, the attacker model generates an adversarial prompt $x$ in line~8 and then attacks the target model to generate response $y$ in line~9. After that, the toxicity score and the behavior of the adversarial prompt and the response are evaluated by the judge models in line~10, and the results are saved in the deep MAP-Elites prioritized replay buffer $\mathcal R$ in line~11, whose process is shown in Algorithm~\ref{alg:replay-buffer-add} of Appendix A.3. For off-policy sampling, an adversarial prompt and its toxicity score and behavior are sampled from the replay buffer $\mathcal R$ in line~13. After data sampling, the reward is calculated with Eq.~(\ref{eq:bc-loss}) and is used to train the attacker model through the arbitrary underlying reinforcement learning algorithm in line~14. After a number of training steps, the attacker models are evaluated in line~16, and the behaviors are reassigned to the attackers based on the evaluation results in line~17.

\input{qdrt}




\paragraph{Behavior assignment.}
Note that it is usually easier to transfer the same attack style to different risk categories. Therefore, we divide the behavior space by the attack style. The simplest behavior assignment strategy is \textbf{random assignment}, which partitions the behavior space uniformly randomly and assigns them to each attacker model at the beginning of the RL stage. To improve the discovery efficiency of diverse behaviors, we further propose the \textbf{adaptive assignment} strategy. As different attacker models may perform better in different sets of behaviors, the random assignment is not efficient. After $T_\mathrm{BR}$ training steps, we evaluate the attacker models to obtain the distribution of attack styles of the prompts generated by each attacker model. Then, we adjust the behavior assignment based on it, assigning each attacker model the attack styles it performs well, and ensuring that the number of attack styles assigned to each attacker models is roughly balanced. Given the attack style distribution $p_i(s) = \mathbb E_{x \sim \pi_{\theta_i}} [p_\psi(s | x)]$ of each attacker model $\pi_{\theta_i}$, we want to find a behavior assignment $\{\mathcal A_i\}_{i=1}^N$ that maximizes $\left\{ \sum_{i=1}^N \mathbb I\left[\{(c, s)\}_{c\in\mathcal C} \subseteq \mathcal A_i\right] p_i(s) \right\}_{s \in \mathcal S}$ and has similar sizes $\{| \mathcal A_i |\}_{i=1}^N$. This optimization problem is not easy to solve and we assign the behaviors roughly in a heuristic way. 
As shown in Algorithm~\ref{alg:behavior} of Appendix A.3, we initialize the behavior assignments as empty sets in line~1 and the set $\mathcal T$ of unassigned attack styles as the whole set of attack styles in line~2. We cyclically iterate over the attacker models, find the unassigned style that is generated the most by the model in line~5, and assign it to the model in line~6. Then, it is removed from $\mathcal T$ in line~7. The iteration stops when all the attack styles are assigned. We reassign the behaviors every $T_{\mathrm{BR}}$ RL training steps to improve the adaptability.

\paragraph{Behavioral replay buffer.}

Prior works~\citep{lee2025learning} use the prioritized replay buffer in the RL training process. When trying to add a prompt $x$ into the buffer, they first ensure the novelty of the prompt by checking whether the prompt has a too small token edit distance or a too small embedding cosine distance compared to existing prompts. Then, the prompt is added into the replay buffer. If the replay buffer is full, the prompt with the lowest quality will be removed.
However, these methods are not effective in maintaining diversity of the replay buffer data.
To enhance the behavioral diversity, QDRT organizes the replay buffer as a deep MAP-Elites archive, i.e., maintains a prioritized replay buffer for each behavior. The process of adding prompt data to the replay buffer is shown in Algorithm~\ref{alg:replay-buffer-add} of Appendix A.3. When trying to add a prompt $x$ into the buffer $\mathcal R = \{\mathcal R_{c,s}\}_{(c,s)\in\mathcal B}$, the risk category $c$ and attack style $s$ of the prompt are first obtained in line~1--2. Then, if the cell (i.e., the sub-replay buffer) $\mathcal R_{c, s}$ corresponding to the risk category $c$ and attack style $s$ is not full, we add the data to the buffer directly in line~4. Otherwise, we find the old prompt data with the lowest toxicity score in line~6. As shown in line~7--8, if the toxicity score of the new data is higher then that of the old data, the old data is replaced by the new data.
When the underlying reinforcement learning algorithm of a attacker model $\pi_{\theta_i}$ requires an off-policy sample from the replay buffer, QDRT first samples a behavior $(c, s)$ from the behavior subspace $\mathcal A_i$ assigned to the attacker model $\pi_{\theta_i}$. Then, QDRT samples replay data from the corresponding replay buffer $\mathcal R_{c,s}$, with higher-toxicity data having a greater probability of being sampled.


\section{Experiments}
\label{sec:experiments}


\subsection{Experimental Settings}
To examine the performance of our proposed QDRT, we conduct experiments on a variety of LLMs, including \texttt{GPT-2}~\cite{long2022training,radford2019language}, \texttt{Llama-3.2-3B-Instruct}~\cite{dubey2024the}, \texttt{Gemma-2-2B-it}~\cite{team2024gemma}, \texttt{Gemma-2-9B-it}~\cite{team2024gemma}, \texttt{Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct}~\cite{dubey2024the}, and \texttt{Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct}~\cite{yang2024qwen2}.
We use \texttt{GPT-2}~\cite{radford2019language} as the base model of the attackers. We use \texttt{Llama-Guard-3}~\cite{inan2023llama} to evaluate the toxicity score $r(x, y)$ and the risk category probabilities $p_{\phi}(c \mid x, y)$ of the attacks $x$ and responses $y$, and instruct a \texttt{Llama-3.2-3B-Instruct} model and OpenAI \texttt{GPT-4.1} to act as the attack style $p_{\psi}(s \mid x)$ judge models. 
The instruction for the attacker models and the attack style evaluator model can be found in Appendix~C.

\paragraph{Dataset and evaluation.}

We consider two types of settings. We first use the benchmark data as the seeds in the training process, use smaller models as target models, and instruct a \texttt{Llama-3.2-3B-Instruct} model as the judge model of attack style. Then, to demonstrate the performance of more complex scenarios, we use augmented data and train the attacker models to attack larger target models, and use OpenAI \texttt{GPT-4.1} as the judge model.


\paragraph{Compared methods.}

Due to the significant costs associated with LLM experiments, we compared basic REINFORCE that optimizes the reward in Eq.~(\ref{eq:reinforce-reward}) as a baseline, as well as the current state-of-the-art generator-level red-teaming algorithm GFlowNets~\cite{lee2025learning}, which demonstrates notably better attacker diversity and fine-tuning safety compared to other recently proposed generator-based red-teaming methods.
QDRT is a general framework and can be implemented with arbitrary reinforcement learning algorithms. In the experiment, we implement a QDRT variant using GFlowNets~\cite{lee2025learning} in RL training. For a fair comparison, all methods use the same number of evaluations and training steps, and the results are reported over three runs.

\paragraph{Metrics.}

To evaluate the performance of the methods, we put the generated adversarial prompts into an MAP-Elites archive.
We mainly consider the following evaluation metrics\footnote{We do not compare the max quality (i.e., max toxicity) of the archive as in the QD works, as it is closed to $1$ as long as there is at least one successful attack, which is common in red-teaming.}: (1) \textbf{QD-Score}: The total sum of the toxicity scores across all prompts in the archive. It measures both the quality and the diversity of the generated prompts. (2) \textbf{Coverage}: The percentage of cells that have been covered by prompts in the archive. It measures the diversity of the generated prompts.


\subsection{Results on Basic Benchmark Dataset}\label{sec:exp-v1}
We first quickly validate the effectiveness of the method on a basic task.
We use the seed prompts from the SafetyDataset~\cite{federico2024safetytuned} and the AdvBench benchmark~\cite{zou2023universal} and train the base attacker model initially with SFT. Then, we train the attacker models with the different methods. In addition, we compare the following ablations of our proposed QDRT: (1) QDRT-Vanilla: Simply maintain several attackers, using the same RL training loss as GFlowNets~\cite{lee2025learning}; (2) QDRT-Vanilla + ME Buffer: Similar to QDRT-Vanilla, but use the deep ME buffer to save the attacks. (3) QDRT-Random: Similar to QDRT, but use random behavior assignment rather than the adaptive behavior assignment. 

As shown in Figure~\ref{fig:ablation-qd-metrics}, QDRT performs the best, obtaining the highest QD-Score and Behavior Coverage. QDRT and QDRT-Random performs better than QDRT-Vanilla + ME Buffer, demonstrating that behavior-conditioned training is helpful for enhancing the diversity of the generated attacks. QDRT-Vanilla + ME Buffer obtains higher QD-Score and coverage than QDRT-Vanilla, showing that the deep ME buffer also helps to maintain the diversity.

