\subsection{Results on Augmented Dataset}\label{sec:exp-v2}
In Section~\ref{sec:exp-v1}, although QDRT’s relative performance is significantly better than the compared methods, the quality of the final attack examples is limited due to the low quality of the initial dataset. Therefore, we performed data augmentation by PAIR on the raw dataset. Based on this augmented data, we compare two SFT approaches: standard cross-entropy loss and the GEM loss~\cite{li2025preserving} that promotes diversity. Then, we ran QDRT and the compared methods and used them to attack three more advanced models: \texttt{Gemma-2-9B-it}, \texttt{Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct}~\cite{dubey2024the}, and \texttt{Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct}~\cite{yang2024qwen2}.

We compare the following approaches: (1) Raw Data: The raw data from SafetyDataset~\cite{federico2024safetytuned} and AdvBench benchmark~\cite{zou2023universal}; (2) Aug. Data: The data augmented by PAIR~\cite{chao2023jailbreaking} and data filtering based on raw data; (3) SFT / GEM~\cite{li2025preserving}: The attacker models fine-tuned with different CE loss and GEM loss, respectively. We use the model fine-tuned by GEM loss as the base model in the RL stage. (4) SFT-$\mathcal B$ / GEM-$\mathcal B$: Similar to SFT / GEM, but use the behavior-conditioned supervised fine-tuning to train the model to generate the adversarial prompts with various behaviors. 

