\section{Conclusion}
\label{sec:conclusion}

Existing red-teaming methods for LLMs face limitations in capturing meaningful diversity of attack strategies and covering diverse risk categories and styles due to simplistic metrics and single-attacker model settings. This paper introduces QDRT to address these issues by defining goal-driven diversity based on a structured behavior space of risk categories and attack styles, employing behavior-conditioned training with multi-specialized attackers, and using an ME replay buffer to balance quality and diversity. Experiments show QDRT generates more diverse and effective attacks with significantly higher toxicity scores and broader coverage across various LLMs like \texttt{Llama-3} and \texttt{Qwen2.5}. By advancing automated red-teaming with systematic diversity optimization, QDRT supports robust LLM safety evaluations.

One possible limitation of our work is the reliance on closed-source commercial APIs for the high-accuracy attack style evaluation, which obscures decision logic, hinders result interpretability, and limits diagnosis of false positives or negatives in adversarial evaluations; leveraging open models (e.g., \texttt{Llama-Guard}~\cite{inan2023llama}) for attack style evaluation by fine-tuning could address these issues. 
Other future works can focus on optimizing behavior allocation, multi-turn attacks~\cite{pavlova2024automated}, and continual learning~\cite{hughes2024position,liu2024autodanturbo,liu2025autort} to enhance its adaptability and scalability.



