\section{Experimental Settings}

\subsection{Experimental Details}

\paragraph{Hyper-parameters.}

For a fair comparison, we train all the base attacker model initially with GEM~\citep{li2025preserving} with \texttt{Llama-Guard-3} as the same judge model to obtain the toxicity reward. We also use the same judge models, i.e. \texttt{Llama-Guard-3} and \texttt{Llama-3.2-3B-Instruct} (or \texttt{GPT-4.1}), for the behavior evaluation. All the settings remain the same across different methods, and are the same as~\cite{lee2025learning}. The detailed hyper-parameters are presented in Table~\ref{tab:hyper-parameters}.

\begin{table}[htbp]
    \centering
    \caption{Hyper-parameters.}
    \label{tab:hyper-parameters}
    \begin{tabular}{l|c}
        \toprule
        Hyper-parameters & Values \\
        \midrule
        Number of attackers & $4$ \\
        Number of risk categories & $14$ \\
        Number of attack styles & $11$ \\
        LoRA rank & $32$ \\
        LoRA alpha & $16$ \\
        Training steps & $5000$ \\
        Steps between behavior reassignment & $400$ \\
        Learning rate & $1 \times 10 ^{-4}$ \\
        Replay buffer size & $5000$ \\
        \bottomrule
    \end{tabular}
\end{table}

\paragraph{Implementation.}
We implement our method based on the code of GFlowNet~\citep{lee2025learning}, and use the ribs~\cite{bryon2021pyribs} library for QD metric computing. The code is available in the supplementary material and will be published once accepted.

\paragraph{Compute Resources.}

All the experiments are conducted on a server with 312 TFLOPs/GPU $\times$ 8 GPUs, AMD EPYC 7713 CPU (64 cores), and 512 GB memory.
Each experiment is conducted on 2 GPUs. In the complex setting, the training process of our proposed QDRT method costs about $48$ hours, while the training process of REINFORCE and GFlowNet costs about $112$ hours. QDRT is more efficient due to its ability of parallelism.


