The application achieves a speedup of $1.32\times$, and although complex-valued arrays have the most impact, more than $3/4$ of the data must be placed in HBM to achieve \SI{90}{\percent} speedup. The requirement to place more data (compared to the benchmarks) in the fast memory to achieve \SI{90}{\percent} speedup can be, at least partially, accounted for by k-Wave being already carefully optimized for the small memory footprint.

\section{Conclusion}

In this article, we introduce a simple tool for analyzing the memory usage of applications on platforms with multiple diverse memory pools. The tool can provide helpful information, such as the usage and performance impact of moving individual allocations between these memory pools. This information can be used by the developer or the tuning tool to optimize mapping between application data and memory pools, thus allowing for efficient use of fast memory of limited size (such as HBM of the SPR platform). 

