\section{Fixed parameter tractability for \BCEOVS{}}

For the \BCEOVS{} problem, the naive algorithm based on branching on the different ways to solve the conflicts generated by geodesics of length 3 is not FPT.
Indeed when there is a geodesic $a,b,c,d$ of length 3 (supposing that $a$ and $c$ are in the set $A$ of the vertices which can be split), we can solve this conflict by either deleting one of the three edges $ab$, $bc$ and $cd$ either by adding the edge $ad$ either by splitting the vertex $c$ (we cannot split the vertex $b$).
As splitting a vertex consists of choosing two subsets of the neighbors of $c$ so that these two subsets cover the neighbors, there are $3^{d(c)}$ possibilities (each vertex can be put in both subsets or in one of the two subsets).
Therefore the number of ways to solve a conflict is not bounded by a function of $k$.

Our idea consists of refining this approach.
As observed for \textsc{Cluster Editing with Vertex Splitting} in \cite{Cygan2015}, all edge editions can be performed before vertex splittings. 
Therefore, when we branch on a split to solve a conflict, we decide the way we split the vertex after having edited edges.
A vertex marked for splitting does not count anymore in search of conflicts (it is like this vertex has been deleted).


\begin{observation}
    Let $G=(V,E)$ be a connected bipartite graph.
    Consider the graph $G'$ obtained from $G$ after a sequence of at most $k$ edge editions or vertex splittings. 
    If $S$ is the set of split vertices, then $G'[V' \setminus S]$ has at most $k+1$ connected components.
\end{observation}
\begin{proof}
    As the graph is initially connected and as an operation can only create at most one new connected component, we deduce that after $k$ operations, the obtained graph has at most $k+1$ connected components.
\end{proof}

At the end of the previous algorithm, when we have done $e$ edge editions and mark $s$ vertices for splitting (with $s+e \leq k$) there is no more conflict in $V \setminus S$.
Thus the graph is $S$ plus a disjoint union of at most $k$ connected graphs.
If these connected graphs are not bicliques, then we return False.
Let $v$ be a vertex of $S$ and $C$ be a connected component of $V \setminus S$ such that $v$ is connected to a vertex of $C$.
Then $v$ should be connected to all the vertices of $C \cap B$ (otherwise any splits of $v$ would not turn $G$ into a disjoint union of bicliques).
We can therefore suppose that for all vertex $v$ of $S$ and all connected component $C$, $v$ is fully connected to $C$ or not connected to $C$.
We denote by $d'(v)$ the number of connected components to which the vertex $v$ is connected.
As there are at most $k+1$ connected components, we deduce that $d'(v) \leq k+1$.

If $v$ is connected to a connected component $C$ having at least one $A$ vertex, then $v$ should be split so that it disconnects $C\cup \{v\}$ to the rest of $S$.
Finally, the remaining connections are between $S$ vertices and connected components having no $A$ vertices (therefore $C$ is a single vertex).



Given a split set $S$ and $C$ and a number of extra splits $e$. 
We consider the algorithm $Aux(S, C, e$) trying all sequences of splits on $S$ of length at most $s + e$ such that each vertex of $S$ is split at least once.
If there exists such a sequence which turns the graph into a disjoint union of 2-clubs, then it returns true.
Otherwise, it returns false.
At the end of such a sequence, the split set is of size at most $2s + e$ (because we create at most $s + e$ copies). 
A split is a choice of a vertex in the current split set and one subset of the vertices for each of the two copies. 
Choosing a neighbors set for one copy corresponds to selecting a subset of the current split set and of the connected components (as a copy must be either adjacent to all vertices of a connected component or not connected to any of these vertices).


\begin{algorithm}[!htb] 
\caption{$Aux(G,S,e)$}
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\Require $G=(A,B,E)$: A bipartite graph
\Require $S$: Subset of $A$
\Require $e$: Number of extra splits
\For{all sequences of splits on $S$ of length at most $|S| + e$ such that each vertex of $S$ is split at least once}
\If{G' is a union of disjoint bicliques} \Return true \EndIf
\EndFor
\State \Return false
\end{algorithmic}
\label{algorithm:aux}
\end{algorithm}

\begin{lemma}   
    The complexity of Algorithm~\ref{algorithm:aux}, $Aux(G,S,e)$, is $O^*(s^{s+e} 4^{s+c})$ where $s$ is the size of $S$ and $c$ is the number of connected components of $G[V \setminus S]$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
    We denote by $s$ the size of $S$.
    There are at most $s^{s+e}$ sequences of vertices of $S$ of length at most $s+e$.
    We denote by $c$ the number of connected components of $G[V \setminus S]$.
    Each split consists of choosing two subsets of vertices of $S$ and two subsets of the connected components of $G[V \setminus S]$.
    We deduce that for one split there are at most $2^s 2^s 2^c 2^c = 4^{s+c}$ choices.
    In total there are at most $s^{s+e} 4^{s+c}$ split sequences examined by the algorithm.
    Applying the sequence to the graph can be done in $O(n^2)$ and checking that the obtained graph is a union of bicliques can be done in $O(n^2)$.
    We deduce the announced complexity.
\end{proof}


\begin{algorithm}[!htb] 
\caption{$BCEOVS(G,S,k)$}
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\Require $G=(A,B,E)$: A bipartite graph
\Require $S$: Subset of $A$
\Require $k$: Remaining number of operations allowed
\If{$k=0$}
    \State \Return $Aux(S, G[V(G) \setminus S], 0)$
\ElsIf{there exists a path $a,b,c,d$ such that $d(a,d) = 3$ and $b \not\in S$ and $c \not\in S$ and $a \in A$} 
    \If{$BCEOVS(G - ab,S, k-1)$}  \Return true \EndIf
    \If{$BCEOVS(G - bc,S, k-1)$}  \Return true  \EndIf
    \If{$BCEOVS(G -cd,S, k-1)$} \Return true    \EndIf
    \If{$BCEOVS(G+ad,S, k-1)$}  \Return true \EndIf
    \If{$BCEOVS(G,S \cup c, k-1)$}  \Return true \EndIf
\Else 
    \State \Return $Aux(S, G[V(G)\setminus S], k)$
\EndIf
\end{algorithmic}
\label{algorithm:main}
\end{algorithm}





\begin{theorem}
\textsc{Bicluster Editing with One-sided Vertex Splitting} can be solved in $O^*(5^{3k} k^{2k})$, where $k$ is the solution size. Therefore, it
is fixed parameter tractable.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
We show that Algorithm~\ref{algorithm:main} returns True if and only if $G$ can be turned into a disjoint union of biclusters with a sequence of length at most $k$ of edge editions and vertex splittings.

As this is a branching algorithm with a branching factor of 5 and as each step is taking $O(n^3)$ to search for a pair of vertices at distance 3 (e.g., with the Floyd-Warshall algorithm), we deduce that the Algorithm~\ref{algorithm:aux} $Aux$ is called at most $O^*(5^k)$ times.
    Each call of $Aux$ has complexity $O^*( k^{2k} 4^{2k})$.
    In total the complexity of Algorithm~\ref{algorithm:main} is at most $O^*(5^k k^{2k} 4^{2k}) = O^*(5^{3k} k^{2k})$.
\end{proof}



\section{A Polynomial-Size Kernel for \textsc{Bicluster Editing with One-sided Vertex Splitting}}

We utilize an equivalent formulation of the \textsc{Bicluster Editing with One-sided Vertex Splitting} problem, where only the vertices of the set $B$ are allowed to split. The objective is to find a cover of the vertices of a bipartite graph $G=(A,B,E)$ such that the restriction to $A$ is a partition.

\begin{definition}
    An $A$-partitioning cover $C$ of a bipartite graph $G=(A,B,E)$ is a set of subsets of $A \cup B$ covering the vertices of $G$ such that the restrictions of the subsets of $C$ to $A$ is a partition of $A$.
    We define the cost of $C$ as follows:
    \begin{itemize}
        \item For every vertex $a \in A$, there exists a unique subset $X$ of $C$ such that $a \in X$ such that the cost of $a$ is defined as $|(B \cap X) \setminus N(a)| + |\overline{X} \cap N(a)|$ (where $\overline{X}$ denotes the complementary of $X$ in $A \cup B$).
        \item For every vertex $b \in B$, the cost of $b$ is defined as $j-1$ where $j$ is the number of subsets of $C$ containing $b$.
    \end{itemize}
    Finally, the cost of $C$ is defined as: $cost(C) = \sum_{a \in A} cost(a) + \sum_{b \in B} cost(b)$.

\end{definition}

The cost of a vertex $a \in A$ will correspond to the number of edited edges incident to $a$ and the cost of a vertex $b \in B$ will correspond to the number of times a vertex $b$ is split.
We will say that the edge $ab$, where $a \in$ will be \textit{deleted} if the unique subset $X$ of $C$ containing $a$ does not contain $b$.


