The observed OOT issue in GNN-ACLP for AnalogGenie may be attributed to the inherent computational complexity of SEAL, particularly due to its node labeling approach \cite{Zhang2022}. Notably, SEAL's performance tends to degrade when applied to highly dense graph structures since its labeling approach struggles to distinguish critical circuit subgraphs \cite{zhang2020revisiting}, a key challenge in AnalogGenie’s highly dense networks.

To address these challenges, we will optimize SEAL’s enclosing subgraph extraction by filtering electrically irrelevant nodes and introduce circuit-specific labeling approach to reduce complexity without sacrificing accuracy in the future. Furthermore, we are exploring alternative frameworks capable of more effective structural representation learning in dense graphs, thereby overcoming SEAL’s limitations in such scenarios.



\section{Conclusion}

We propose GNN-ACLP, a novel graph neural networks based framework with SEAL framework integration and achieve port-level accuracy in analog circuit link prediction. We also propose Netlist Babel Fish, a netlist format conversion tool combining RAG with an LLM. Additionally, we create the SpiceNetlist dataset for training and evaluation of circuit link prediction methods. Results of the experiment show that our method achieves state-of-the-art performance. 

