Stochastic computing (SC) approximates complex arithmetic operations, such as addition and multiplication, using a single bitwise operation, like \texttt{majority} or \texttt{AND}, on large bit streams.
Although SC is inherently fault-tolerant, its accuracy largely depends on the size and quality of the stochastic bit streams, which are typically generated by CMOS-based pseudo-random number generators (PRNGs) and consume over 80\% of the system's energy and area.
Current SC solutions focus on optimizing the logic gates and often neglect the high cost of moving the bit streams.%, which can easily become a bottleneck. 
