\section{Background and Motivation}
\subsection{ReRAM-based Computing}

Redox-based resistive random access memory (ReRAM)~\cite{dittmann2021nanoionic} is a two-terminal variable resistor with an oxide layer between two metal electrodes. The 1T1R (one transistor and one ReRAM) crossbar has been widely explored as a matrix-vector multiplier to accelerate neural networks (NNs). However, its potential is limited by reliability challenges and the energy-intensive digital-to-analog (DAC) and analog-to-digital (ADC) conversions required.

An alternative approach for 1T1R arrays is stateful logic, such as MAGIC and IMPLY~\cite{}. However, these are incompatible with CMOS logic and limited by device endurance, as outputs are always represented as resistance states in memristors. In contrast, non-stateful designs like scouting logic~\cite{yu2019enhanced} offer a solution by enabling functionally complete Boolean logic while reducing endurance requirements and improving performance. In scouting logic, inputs are represented by the resistive states of memristors, and outputs are derived from the current during simultaneous readout. By adjusting the reference current, logical functions such as AND, OR, and XOR can be executed efficiently.

