\subsection{Prior Work: SC in Memory}

\blue{Knag et al.~\cite{SC_InMemory_Native2014} developed a hybrid system of memristors integrated with CMOS-based stochastic circuits. The BSs are generated in memory, but the computations are performed off-memory using CMOS stochastic circuits. Finally, the output BSs are written back to the memristive memory. Expanding the effort in~\cite{SC_InMemory_Native2014}, the authors in~\cite{IMC_SC_DNN_2021} exploited the well-known switching stochasticity of probabilistic \gls{CBRAM} devices to efficiently generate stochastic BSs in memory. They use the generated BSs to optimize deep learning parameters using a hybrid CMOS-memristor stochastic processor.
A flow-based in-memory \gls{sc} architecture is proposed in~\cite{SC_Memristor_2017}. The design exploits the flow of current through probabilistically-switching memristive nano switches in high-density crossbars to perform SC. They represent data using bit-vector stochastic streams of varying bit-widths instead of traditional stochastic streams composed of individual bits. A physics-based probabilistic switching model for ReRAM stochastic BS generation is developed in~\cite{Physic_RRAM_SC_SNG_2019}. Gupta et al.~\cite{SCRIMP_DATE20} developed SCRIMP, an architecture for \gls{sc} acceleration with ReRAM in-memory processing. Riahi Alam et al.~\cite{RiahiAlam_DT2021_} developed an exact (completely accurate) method for \gls{sc} multiplication in memristive memory. To this end, they proposed a method for deterministic and accurate binary to BS conversion in memory. Sun et al.~\cite{Unary_IMC_2021} employed unary coding, implemented with multi-level memristor cells, for weight representation in a ReRAM-based NN design. They apply unary coding to tolerate the device resistance variations and design accurate ReRAM-based NN accelerators.
