\section{\blue{Proposed} In-RRAM Stochastic Computing}
In this section, we demonstrate how binary data can be locally converted into stochastic bit-streams using the random source from the previous section and bulk-bitwise operations in ReRAM. We show that all steps can be performed within ReRAM, making stochastic computing (SC) an efficient and compatible method for in-memory computing with unreliable cells.

\subsection{RNG using read disturbance}
The inherent variability of the ReRAM devices allows for the generation of random based on different properties \cite{Woo2019, Tseng2021, Pellerin2019}. Here, the state instability of the TaO$_{\text{X}}$-based devices is utilized. Debating opinions exist on whether this read instability is caused by the trapping and detrapping of electrons~\cite{Puglisi2014} or by the relocation of oxygen vacancies \cite{Funck2021001}. The read instability is recorded by applying a constant 0.2 V bias to the device.

For the generation of random numbers used in this paper, the read instability is split, and one-half is delayed by an RC circuit. Subsequently the delayed and not delayed signals are compared. The output of this comparison already meets the requirements of the NIST test. As the typical frequency of read instability is not higher than 10000 Hz, the bit generation rate of this approach is limited. Here, the generation rate is increased by applying the result of the comparison along with a clock signal to an and gate to generate the seed for a 4-bit nonlinear feedback shift register. Thus, a generation rate of 35 kbit can be reached \cite{Schnieders_2024}.  


\subsection{Stochastic number generation (SNG)}

SC represents real numbers probabilistically through bit-streams, where the occurrence rates of 1s and 0s correspond to the real values. While hardware random number generators (RNGs) based on CMOS are costly and occupy significant chip area, pseudo-random number generators (PRNGs) like linear-feedback shift registers (LFSRs) are faster and simpler but prone to random variations, leading to inaccuracies. Deterministic methods, such as using decoders or look-up tables (LUTs), avoid these inaccuracies but are still expensive when implemented in CMOS. In in-memory computing, including costly peripherals for SNGs is undesirable, as they can create energy and performance bottlenecks, much like ADCs do in matrix-vector multiplications in 1T1R arrays.


Instead, we propose generating stochastic bitstreams through in-memory bitwise operations. First, random numbers are first generated and stored in dedicated rows, which can be reused across applications. If multiple independent streams are needed, additional rows can be allocated. These random numbers are then used by the SNG to produce stochastic bitstreams using in-memory comparisons. The greater-than operation $a\_i > b\_i$ is broken down into bitwise comparisons, starting from the MSB to the LSB, detecting when  $a\_i > b\_i$ at the first position (i.e., where $a\_i \oplus b\_i$ is 1), aided by a flag. This process is described in terms of Boolean operations and converted into an XOR-AND graph (XAG), a logic network of AND gates, XOR gates, and inverters, which is optimized logic synthesis tools like mockturtle~\cite{} to minimize gate count. Finally, the XAG network is mapped to the Boolean operations supported by the memory sense amplifier.


Table~\ref{tab:mse_data_conversion} presents the Mean Squared Error (MSE) values for converting unsigned integers using the following SNG methods: software-based comparison (SW) using NumPy's PRNs, LFSR, and in-memory SNG (IMSNG) using ReRAM-based random numbers while accounting for bit-flips errors due to ReRAM variability.
The data is based on 1 million samples drawn from a uniform distribution over the range $[1, 2^b-1]$, where $b$ denotes the number of bits of the integer input and $sb$ is the bitstream length. 
The results highlight trade-offs among these methods across different bit precisions used in image processing and machine learning. IMSNG, despite its random fluctuations, generally reduces conversion errors compared to PRNs. LFSRs, however, better mitigate these fluctuations, offering more stability in the conversion process. In the next section, we examine the impact of the in-memory stochastic computing method at the arithmetic operation level.


