\subsection{Stochastic Circuits using Scouting Logic}

Previous works have proposed cost-effective schemes for implementing logic operations in memory peripheral circuits.
The most promising Boolean logic designs are based on read operation and evaluate the logic output value by a modified sense amplifier~\cite{pinatubo,yu2019enhanced}. 
During a logic operation, two or more rows are simultaneously activated and the resulting current through the cells in each bitline is compared with a reference current $I_{ref}$ by
the sense amplifier, whose output is the desired result of the Boolean operation. 
Hence, performing logic operations with no impact on device endurance. 
Several Boolean logic designs based on a modified sense amplifier support a subset of basic operations such as (N)AND, (N)OR, X(N)OR, and NOT. For a robust and efficient model, we adopt Scouting Logic (SL)~\cite{yu2019enhanced}, which enables these operations in a single cycle. 
%However, the equivalent resistance of two (or more) parallel cells considering variability makes it difficult to set a reference RA to distinguish between the logic '1' (both cells in LRS) and logic '0' (cells in different states or both cells in HRS) in the AND operation, i.e., assuming that a logic one and zero are represented by low and high resistance. 


Bulk bitwise in-memory operations are performed on large vectors. When operating with binary arithmetic, we can only exploit the SIMD parallelism, since these algorithms are sequential by nature due to carry propagation. 
Unlike positional numeral systems, SC handles the basic arithmetic (addition, subtraction, multiplication, division) with very simple, low-cost logic units, such as AND/NOT, multiplexers and flip-flops, where each bit is often computed independently.
This allows in-memory SC to exploit bulk-bitwise logic and massive word-level parallelism, computing all bit positions simultaneously and significantly reducing latency for basic arithmetic.
Here, we explain how these arithmetic operations work in bulk bitwise logic schemes.

Multiplication in SC is implemented by two independent bitstreams, representing probabilities the $p$ and $q$, that are multiplied by an AND operation. The output stream's probability of a '1' equals $p \land q$, which aligns well with the principles of SL with the time complexity of $\mathcal{O}(1)$. 
This contrasts with conventional bulk-bitwise implementations of integer multiplication, which exhibit a time complexity of $\mathcal{O}(n^2)$, where $n$ represents the number of bits. This complexity arises from the iterative nature of traditional methods, which rely on bit shifts and additions to compute the product.


Scaled additions in SC are effectively implemented using multiplexers in CMOS designs.
Specifically, $y = x_1 x^{\prime}_w + x_2 x_w$ represents a scaled addition, where $x_w$ is a scaling factor. When $x_w = 0.5$, the output is halved, a simplification that is often acceptable in various applications.
In SL, 2-to-1 multiplexers can be approximated by 3-input majority (MAJ) gates~\cite{chen2015equivalence}, which provide a hardware-friendly execution as MAJ can be implemented in fewer cycles compared to the conventional multiplexer logic. 
For that, a reference current that corresponds to the majority of the inputs being set to one is required. For instance, in a 3-input majority gate (MAJ-3), the same reference current used for a 2-input AND gate can be employed, as this configuration will detect when at least two out of three inputs are high.
The time complexity of this MAJ-based addition is $\mathcal{O}(1)$, which is a significant improvement over traditional ripple-carry additions that have a time complexity of $\mathcal{O}(n)$. 
To implement scaled subtraction, we can employ a similar approach as in scaled addition, but with the inclusion of the NOT gate to obtain $1 - P(x_2)$. This inversion is accomplished by reading the negated value, denoted as $\overline{bitline}$, from the sense amplified. 

Division in SC typically uses CMOS-based flip-flops and multiplexers for approximating $y = x_1 / x_2$ with correlated bitstreams.
In SL, the intermediate values from the flip-flop can either be stored as resistance states in memory or forwarded to the bitline as voltage inputs, which helps preserve device endurance. This method has a time complexity of $\mathcal{O}(n)$, offering a more efficient alternative to the schoolbook long division method currently used in in-memory computing, which requires $\mathcal{O}(n^2)$ cycles.


%\blue{[Here we can add a table to report the accuracy (mean absolute error) of our technique for basic SC operations.]}

Additionally, parallel implementations of stochastic operators have been developed to further enhance performance. These designs allow for the simultaneous processing of multiple bitstreams. 

Stochastic numbers involve a typical storage overhead of $\mathcal{O}(2^n)$ for representing an n-bit real value with minimal accuracy loss. However, the bitstream length can be adjusted as a form of quantization, which is acceptable for many use cases~\cite{}. In addition, 1T1R ReRAM arrays are highly dense, and the trade-off of using more arrays is often justified by eliminating the need for ADCs.

