\subsection{Optimizations}

\noindent \textbf{Device and Architecture}
\begin{itemize}
\item techniques to hide the RNG latency: random offset for memory addressing
\item hide the generation of stochastic stream: optimized comparator
\item when is it possible to reuse random vectors? can we do some DFG analysis to identify them? 
\item review the papers that propose in-memory SC computing (from 2015 or so)
\item multiplication and addition comparison to other in-memory binary computation (dot-products with ADCs and MAGIC-based logic)
\end{itemize}

\noindent\textbf{DSE (or cross-layer design) of in-memory greater-than comparison:}
\begin{itemize}
\item Implementing a full-fledged comparator logic as a peripheral. Should be faster, but increased area and energy. Requires a single write op for storing the SB back in the memory
\item  Comparison using bulk bitwise operations only. It requires 2 writes per input bit. It results in smallest area, but slower and energy inefficient, also bad for endurance
\item Bulk-bitwise comparison using a dual-latch row buffer scheme for eliminating write operations. The scouting logic's sense amplifier requires 15T + an explicit latch (8T) for buffering. By introducing an additional latch (8T) and a mux (6T), we can do the comparison in memory with read operations only. 
\end{itemize}

\noindent \textbf{General costs to be considered when proposing a custom SA / row buffer:}
\begin{itemize}
\item Latch with enable = 8T
\item MUX = 12T/2T  (CMOS/PTL)
\item SL-SA = 9T+XOR = 15T
\item XOR = 4T/6T
\item NVM SA should consider a write driver for avoiding writing where the value is already set.  Write driver = 3T + latch (8T). We could somehow use the same latch for write-mask and for implementing CIM with intermediate values.
\end{itemize}

